WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.007 --> 00:00:02.380
- Did you ever do a mistake at work?

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.610
Surely, I did lots of mistakes

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.510
during the course of my career.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.200
Many of them when I was young

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.270
in the first years after graduating.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:12.820
But for sure, even today as well.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.440
And I would argue that there's
hardly any senior manager

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.770
in any business role
that you could talk to,

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.350
who would not have happened
at least one or two

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:24.370
or three major incidents, major mistakes,

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:27.370
major problems that they were more or less

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.030
directly responsible for.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.880
Mistakes at work are therefore
nothing to be ashamed of.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.240
The question is rather
how do you deal with them

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.570
if they happen and how do you
avoid them for happening again

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:38.580
in the future?

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.030
So this is exactly the topic
I'm going to talk about today.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.990
So welcome to another coffee break here

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:45.700
on my channel, Firm Learning.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:46.750
My name is Heinrich,

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.340
and on my channel I want to
help you to become successful

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.980
in the first years of your career.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.220
I'm going to address this
topic in three steps.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.780
First, I'm going to share
some typical mistakes

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.050
that might often happen to some juniors,

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.140
people in the first years of their career.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.470
Second, I'm going to
share some strategies,

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.130
what you can do if a
mistake happened to you.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:07.580
And third, let me share
some tips and tricks

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.110
on what you can do to
actually avoid mistakes

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.370
in the first place.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.150
So make sure you watch until the end

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:14.850
to see these strategies,

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.870
which I find really quite helpful

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:18.460
for your daily life as well.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:21.330
One of the tips I will talk
about is using checklists

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.470
for repeated tasks that you
will need to do frequently.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.690
And here, actually I have a
checklist that I used myself

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:30.190
while I worked at
McKinsey prepared for you.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:33.090
So you can find it via a link
in the video description,

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:33.923
check it out.

00:01:33.923 --> 00:01:36.800
So let's start talking about
what some mistakes could be

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.170
that might happen to
you in your daily job.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.300
And frankly, these are all
things that happened to myself.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.600
So I did these mistakes in the past

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.830
and surely there are
many, many other things

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.410
that you might do wrong at
some point in your career.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.310
So first example: You are
asked to bring handouts

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:54.120
of a document to the meeting,

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.350
but you actually forgot to
print out the documents in time.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.330
Now you're sitting in the
meeting without handouts,

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.500
everybody's looking at you

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.780
and you need to admit that
you did not bring them.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.963
Next, you created an Excel
analysis with some client data,

00:02:05.963 --> 00:02:07.940
with some information that you received,

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.780
and then you shared some
results from that analysis

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.520
with the client or with colleagues.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.710
And then at some point you
find out that some formulas

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:18.400
in the model were wrong, that
your model had a problem.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.300
And likely, the data that
you shared is now inaccurate,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.740
potentially even significantly inaccurate.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.540
The truth is, this likely happens
more often than you think.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.360
I remember a partner
saying that she believes

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.300
that likely there is not a
single complex Excel model

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.580
that is completely error-free.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.730
So if something like this happens to you,

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.620
trust that you will not be the only one.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.680
Or maybe you are asked
a question in a meeting

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.650
and then you share the wrong information

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.560
because you misremembered it.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:46.620
So here, one example:

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.080
I remember that in one past project,

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.310
I was once asked for the savings potential

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:53.600
that we calculated for a specific measure

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:54.640
that we were working on.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.210
And I believe I said something like, yeah,

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.070
the savings potential is 20 million Euro,

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.870
but in the end it came out
that it was only 5 million

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.370
because I just mixed it up

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.290
with something else that we calculated.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:06.123
But in the meantime,

00:03:06.123 --> 00:03:08.250
this number was already communicated.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.540
This person has said this to,

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.640
communicated this further to other people

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:12.860
in the organization.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:16.270
So surely this was quite
an unpleasant situation

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.103
when then it came out

00:03:17.103 --> 00:03:19.010
that this number was not actually correct.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.150
Along these lines, of course,

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:21.960
many other things can go wrong as well.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.690
Maybe you missed the
deadline for a piece of work

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.020
that you needed to submit,

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.617
or you actually have some
spelling or grammar mistakes

00:03:28.617 --> 00:03:30.360
in any of your documents.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.510
All of these are just different examples

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.280
of mistakes that could happen to you.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.150
And let me share one more
anecdote of what once happened

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.620
in a consulting project.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.300
So we had a big workshop

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.120
and an associate was responsible

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.741
for preparing this workshop.

00:03:43.741 --> 00:03:46.220
And the workshop was a lot
about product line strategy,

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.060
lots of numbers prepared for that.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.750
There were board level members
attending this workshop.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.570
We as a consulting firm
had also partners flying in

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.990
for the meeting, attending it,

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.770
because it just was so
important to the project

00:03:58.770 --> 00:03:59.800
that we were conducting.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.370
It was quite a complex session.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:02.990
There were numbers printed out

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.400
on the wall on big wallpapers.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.810
Then we were supposed to
discuss with the clients,

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:09.460
and after starting this discussion,

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:12.770
about 20 minutes into the
workshop it became clear

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:15.140
that all the numbers that were prepared,

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.470
that were presented,

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.260
were actually wrong.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.650
So apparently that
associate pulled numbers

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.110
from a wrong financial year
or something like that.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:25.020
And unfortunately,

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.710
no one noticed until then at some point,

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.970
one of the clients noticed that.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:33.240
And surely this was extremely unfortunate

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.410
and surely also extremely
embarrassing for us

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:37.290
as consultants preparing the session.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.660
So having said that,

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.500
what should you do if
actually a mistake like this

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:42.667
happens to you?

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:44.550
And one thing that really annoys me

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.150
from the perspective of a manager

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.890
is when I have the
perception that the person

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.950
who did a mistake does
not really acknowledge it,

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:55.260
does not really understand
that the person made a mistake.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.080
Doesn't really seem to care

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.230
that that person did something wrong.

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:03.290
So while it is not necessary
to apologize for your mistakes,

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:04.937
so also would not recommend that you say,

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:07.420
"Sorry, sorry, sorry that I did that."

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.400
You should at least acknowledge that, yes,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.850
you did something wrong.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:12.080
That, yes, you understand

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.100
that these things should not happen.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:17.140
That, yes, you will work on
this not happening again.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.020
Because if you don't do or say anything

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:20.230
after you did a mistake,

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.140
your team might get the perception

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.670
that you don't really care.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.630
Or that for you it's not even important

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.560
that your documents are without spelling

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:28.750
or grammar mistakes.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:31.160
That these things do not
really matter to you.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:32.610
And this is surely a perception

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:34.230
that you in general want to avoid.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:35.590
What's important is where it goes

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:38.110
to always keep things in perspective.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:39.970
So surely a spelling mistake

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.240
or not bringing your
handouts to the meeting,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.260
while it's not great,

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.220
it's not the end of the world.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.620
So I sometimes see
especially junior people

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:51.530
really beating themselves
up over really minor things.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:53.490
While, of course, these
things are not great,

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:55.800
they likely have happened
to everyone at some point

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:56.790
in their career.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:59.280
And surely this is nothing to be scared

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.800
or to not sleep well at
night or anything like that.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:02.880
But sometimes, of course,

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.810
also more severe, more
significant things go wrong,

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.020
like in the example with a client workshop

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:09.010
that I talked about.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.970
Surely the associate who
prepared the workshop

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:13.130
will not feel great about this,

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:15.610
and probably should really
reflect and introspect

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.290
on what happened, what
led to the situation,

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.970
why this mistake was done.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:21.220
When in situations like this,

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.450
it's also a question of good leadership,

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.620
to not only see the
mistake at the associate

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:28.040
or at the junior colleague

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:29.940
or at the person that
actually prepared the things

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:30.820
in the very end.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.270
Because in the workshop
example it was definitely

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.700
also the failure of the project lead

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:37.330
to not double-check the numbers,

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:39.040
to conduct some quality checks

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:40.640
on the works of the associates.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:43.930
And it surely was also the
responsibility of the partner

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:46.800
to not really work with the
team, challenge the team,

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.100
to make sure in the preparatory call

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:50.550
that definitely happened,

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.100
make sure that everything is correct

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.570
and the documents are in order.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.210
Especially in more complex
situations that go wrong,

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.937
while it might sometimes be
easy to just point to one person

00:06:58.937 --> 00:07:00.480
who did something wrong,

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.700
it is often rather the
failure of the whole team.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.610
Different failures in how
different levels of the team

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:09.440
should collaborate and
should quality check the work

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.480
that then in total led to these issues.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:13.510
What's always important
if you do a mistake

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:15.640
is to actually be open
and honest about it.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.870
And here, again, let me
share one anecdote from work.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:20.460
There was once a situation

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:22.380
where a project lead was sitting together

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:23.360
with a board member,

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.620
and then also one or two
other partners around.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.640
And then the board member asked the team,

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.430
asked the project lead
whether he already reached out

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:31.620
to another client,

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:33.270
a colleague of the board member,

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:36.490
to circulate and talk about
the results with that person.

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:38.510
And in that situation,
the project lead said,

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:39.740
yes, he did that, right?

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:40.890
He talked to that person,

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.360
he circulated this information
already with the person.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:44.200
But for whatever reason,

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.200
the board member was a
bit skeptical about that.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:47.430
So in the meeting,

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.740
the board member called this client

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:52.220
and asked the person
whether such an interaction,

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:53.970
such a meeting actually took place.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:56.530
And it came out that this did not happen,

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:57.590
so that frankly,

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:00.830
the project lead lied to the board member.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:03.250
And probably the project
lead did not even intend

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:04.370
anything bad.

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:05.963
Likely he just wanted to cover up,

00:08:05.963 --> 00:08:07.870
that probably he did not do this,

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:09.250
which likely he should should have done.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:11.850
Probably he would have directly
after the meeting reach out

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:14.180
to this other person and
then share the numbers,

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.060
share the data to get this in order.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:17.320
So while at that moment,

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:19.340
to the project lead
this likely just seemed

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:21.210
to be a little minor cover up,

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:24.210
the damage that then
resulted from the situation

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:25.740
was of course significant.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.160
The trust between the board
member and the consulting team

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:29.320
was directly gone.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.720
And certainly the project lead also faced

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:34.220
internal consequences after this incident.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:36.440
So while sometimes you might
get away with something,

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.530
I would really recommend you
to make it a habit of yours

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:42.140
of always saying the truth, be honest,

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:43.840
be open about what happened.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.800
Don't try dodgy things,
covering things up,

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.470
because it will come
to situations like this

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.320
where things can really, really hurt you,

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:52.630
hurt your career,

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:54.650
hurt, of course, the
reputation of your firm,

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:55.730
of your employer,

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:58.130
and this is usually not
worth it in the end.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:01.800
Now let me share one maybe
a little bit sneaky hack

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.780
that I once learned from a project lead

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:05.350
whenever you did something wrong.

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:07.730
And from this project lead
I learned the principle

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:11.210
that you should share good
news always in writing,

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.850
but bad news only verbally, right?

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:15.670
So good news in writing,

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:18.780
bad news when you did
something wrong, only verbally.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:21.660
And the reason for that is that
if you did something great,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:22.700
you can write about this.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:23.930
Then maybe your team lead,

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:26.500
other members of your team
receive this as an email,

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:27.440
for instance.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:29.390
They can potentially forward it around,

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:32.060
forward it to to other
people in the organization.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:34.110
So more people learn about what you did,

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:36.280
about the great things that happen.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:38.110
Surely this is what you want.

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:40.650
Though the other way around
this is what you might avoid

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:42.759
when you actually did something wrong,

00:09:42.759 --> 00:09:44.890
when you did a mistake,
when you did an error.

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:47.320
So yes, be open and honest about it.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.490
Maybe give a call to your team lead

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:50.850
to disclose what happened,

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.860
to think about what
the next steps could be

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.600
to rectify the situation.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.620
But try to do this verbally,
in person or on the phone,

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:01.110
but do not put this
information in an email,

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:03.840
because, again, if it
is written in an email,

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:06.720
then that person might forward
it to other team members.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.400
Then more and more colleagues
might learn about that,

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:11.090
or maybe half a year later,

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:13.920
that person is going
over his or her inbox.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:16.200
And then suddenly this
email pops up again.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:17.950
And they remember the situation,

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:19.500
they remember this mistake,

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:21.670
this error that you did at the time,

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:23.570
which they might already have forgotten

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:25.250
if you just shared it with them verbally.

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:28.910
So share good news in writing
and bad news only verbally.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:31.450
So I agree that this might
be a little bit sneaky,

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:33.530
but this still appears
to be fair game to me

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.200
and just a little hack that
you can use in your daily work.

